  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nAs of 3AM Tuesday morning, an expansive midlevel ridge continues to \nreside from the central Rockies into he central/southern \nPlains. A shortwave trough was sliding through the upper Midwest\nwith an attendant surface trough extending from northern MI \nthrough northeast MO and into northern KS. Ahead of the front a \nwarm and moist boundary layer remains in the place with \ntemperatures still in the mid-80s across central and northern KS\nand dew points in the lower 70s. This frontal zone will \ncontinue to sink south through the morning and afternoon. By \nmidday, the front is expected to reside near the KS/OK border, \nwhich is a few hours faster than prior model suites suggested. \nDespite the slightly faster progression, areas generally along \nand south of Highway 50 will see the overlap of hot temperatures\nand humid conditions, at least briefly. Heat indices (up to 107\ndegrees) will peak across portions Reno, Harvey, Kingman, \nSedgwick, Butler, Greenwood, Woodson, and Allen counties by \nearly afternoon with improving heat indices into the late\nafternoon hours. Areas closer to the KS/OK border will see a \nmore-significant overlap of hot temperatures and humid \nconditions. Latest short term guidance suggests, dew points will\nremain in the low to mid 70s during the early and mid- \nafternoon hours. These will overlap with temperatures nearing \nthe 100 degree mark, contributing to heat indices up to 115. \nHeat indices will improve by early evening as the drier low- \nlevel air filters into southern KS. As a result, there hasn't \nbeen any changes to the inherited Heat Advisory or Extreme Heat\nWarning. Aside from the heat, a stray thunderstorm cannot be\nentirely ruled out across far southern KS and within the \nfrontal zone. Steepening low-level lapse rates combined with \nweak convergence along the frontal zone may prove just \nsufficient for convective initiation. Should a storm develop, \nbrief heavy rainfall and gusty winds will be possible. Again \nthis potential is mainly confined to near the KS/OK border.\n\nTransitioning into tonight, thunderstorms are likely to develop this \nafternoon within an upslope flow regime across eastern WY. As \nthe evening and overnight progresses, these storms are likely \nto grow upscale and move into portions of central NE. There \nremains a slight chance this complex of storms could reach \nportions of north-central KS after sunrise Wednesday morning. \n\nThe potential for nocturnal convection increases late Wednesday\nnight into Thursday morning as a strong low-level and midlevel \nWAA pattern emerges from western NE through portions of central \nand eastern KS. Scattered showers and thunderstorms may persist \nthrough much of the day Thursday, especially across northeast KS\nwhere weak WAA will continue. \n\nAnother complex of thunderstorm activity is appearing more and more \nlikely late Thursday into Friday. A shortwave trough is forecast to \neject into the central Plains Thursday evening into Thursday night. \nScattered thunderstorm development is expected along an inverted \ntrough axis across eastern CO/western KS/western NE. As the evening \nprogresses, convection is likely to grow upscale into an MCS and \ntranslate eastward within a 35-45 kt LLJ. The greatest storm chances \nwill remain across portions of central and northeast KS. Additional \nstorms are possible Friday night into Saturday morning as the \nsurface trough slides across southeast KS. The core of the midlevel \nridge appears to slide back over the central Plains by next weekend. \nThis will likely yield warmer temperatures, heat indices near/above \n100 degrees, and minimal rain chances from Saturday afternoon into \nnext week.",
